---
description: Collisions are prevented by structure, not judgment. Ownership manifest as data, brokered resources, two-phase waves under frozen contracts, computed writer budget with no fixed cap. Wins over any "max agents" or "asap" instruction. Only the operator can weaken it.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Structural Concurrency (HARD RULE)

A rule an agent can violate is advice. Make the wrong action impossible.
"Max agents" / "asap" / "as fast as possible" means: reach a correct, verified
result in the least wall-clock time. It changes how wide the independent work
runs. It never relaxes single-owner, no-self-approval, or verified-before-
landing. More agents must never make the system worse.

## Ownership manifest (disjointness as data)
`OWNERSHIP.yaml` at repo root maps partition ids to path globs and owned
resources (ports, build dirs, schemas, services). Create it on the first wave;
refine it as the codebase teaches you. Rules:
- A write task claims exactly ONE partition (declared in its ledger entry).
- Any diff outside the claimed partition is auto-rejected. Enforce with a
  pre-commit hook where present; until the hook exists, the writer self-checks
  the diff against the manifest before every commit and the reviewer re-checks.
- Two partitions that both modify a shared upstream (same imported type, same
  schema, same generated code) are NOT disjoint. Fix the manifest, not the
  judgment call.

## Resource broker (allocated, never hand-picked)
Ports, build output dirs, simulators/emulators, and local DBs are taken by
writing a lease file `.agents/leases/<resource>.lease` containing holder, pid,
heartbeat timestamp, and expiry. Never start a server or build on a hand-picked
port or dir. Stale lease (no heartbeat past expiry): supervisor reclaims; no
one else. Read the repo's own scripts for real ports and commands; never invent
a competing dev/build command. Before starting a server or build, check the
lease dir, not just the process list.

## Two-phase waves (this is what makes wide safe)
- Phase 0, contract freeze: ONE owner lands ALL shared-contract changes for the
  wave: types, schemas, API and websocket contracts, routers, codegen. Then the
  contracts are frozen for the wave.
- Phase 1, fan out: writers run wide under the frozen contracts, each inside
  one partition. Under a frozen contract, disjointness is provable and cheap.
- A contract change needed mid-wave = stop the wave, re-plan, re-freeze. Never
  patch a shared contract in parallel with consumers of it.

## Writer budget (computed, not capped)
Per wave: `writers = min(proven_partitions, review_lanes, merge_budget,
infra_capacity)`. State the binding constraint in the plan line before
dispatch, e.g. "18 partitions proven, review lanes 18, merge budget 22, infra
40: dispatch 18, bound by partitions." Rules:
- No writer without a proven partition. Fewer writers is correct when the work
  does not split. There is no fixed numeric cap and no target count.
- Read-only agents (audit, grep, review, test-running, scanning): unlimited.
- Collision check before every dispatch: candidate needs a held resource =
  serialize or defer. Never dispatch into a collision.

## Forever-serial resources (single owner at ANY writer count)
| Resource | Rule |
|---|---|
| Native iOS/Android sync + signing | One owner per wave |
| Store / TestFlight upload | One owner per build number |
| Production deploy | One owner per environment |
| Migrations on a shared database | One migration owner |
| Git destructive ops (rebase/reset/clean/force-push) | One owner |
| Package install per root | One install owner |
| Dev server port / long-running process | One owner per port/process |

## Freeze on breakage
When a shared resource or the trunk is broken: stop dispatching writers, follow
the containment protocol (05), resume only after end-to-end green.

## Changing this rule
Do NOT edit, weaken, or override without explicit operator approval. If a task
seems to require it, STOP and ask, stating the change and why. Repo-specific
rules may ADD stricter constraints, never relax these.
